<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<aiml version="2.0">
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT IS KUHUL</pattern>
    <template>In this repository, K'UHUL is a typed semantic runtime and language stack. It organizes typed keys and fields into bounded Micronaut programs, advances work through Pop, Wo, Yax, Sek, Ch'en, and Xul, and leaves host capability admission to runtime contracts and XCFE.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT IS K UHUL</pattern>
    <template>In this repository, K'UHUL is a typed semantic runtime and language stack. It organizes typed keys and fields into bounded Micronaut programs, advances work through Pop, Wo, Yax, Sek, Ch'en, and Xul, and leaves host capability admission to runtime contracts and XCFE.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>DEFINE KUHUL</pattern>
    <template>K'UHUL is a typed semantic runtime that resolves key identities and fields, sequences bounded Micronaut work through six gated phases, and leaves capability admission to the host and XCFE boundary.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>HOW DOES KUHUL WORK</pattern>
    <template>A request enters Pop, Wo binds intent and constraints, Yax expands candidates, Sek selects or proposes dispatch, Ch'en verifies results, and Xul closes and records admitted work. Phase labels alone do not authorize execution.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT ARE THE KUHUL PHASES</pattern>
    <template>The phases are Pop for ingress and provenance, Wo for context and typed bindings, Yax for candidate expansion, Sek for selection or dispatch proposals, Ch'en for verification, and Xul for closing and recording admitted work.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT ARE KUHUL SEMANTIC TRACKS</pattern>
    <template>Semantic tracks describe governed concepts, capabilities, and conformance questions. They can guide intent matching and related-key retrieval. After an AIML pattern anchors the question, the runtime can compare similar key and n-gram patterns, unfold relevant context, and rank candidates. Similarity retrieves context; admitted sources support factual claims.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>DO SEMANTIC TRACKS GUIDE AIML</pattern>
    <template>Yes. Tracks can guide which AIML pattern or intent to consider. Related keys and n-grams can then expand and rank relevant context. Similarity is a retrieval signal, not evidence; factual claims still need an admitted source.</template>
  </category>
</aiml>
